      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright© by the American Society for Clinical Pathology (ASCP).",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate the potential role of IL-1A gene variations in pathogenesis of coronary artery disease (CAD) with familial history."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "We investigated the IL-1A-889C>T polymorphism in 335 patients with CAD and 335 healthy individuals for case-control association analysis. In this study, we also investigated the heritability of the susceptible allele from 130 trio families with CAD-affected offspring. We performed genotyping using the polymerase chain reaction-restriction fragment length polymorphism (PCR-RFLP) method."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The IL-1A-889 C/T polymorphism was significantly associated with CAD in patients compared with healthy controls. The minor allele T at -889 was more prevalent in cases vs controls. The results of a transmission disequilibrium test revealed a significant association between the IL-1A-889 polymorphism and CAD. The mutant genotype CT+TT was significantly associated with high levels of high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (CRP) and other relative markers from patients with CAD."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "For the first time in the literature, to our knowledge, we demonstrate a significant association of the IL-1A-889 functional polymorphism with CAD."
          }
        ]
      },
